## sn1.20
sn1.20:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
sn1.20:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha in the Hot Springs Monastery. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Rājagaha at Tapodā monastery.
sn1.20:1.3 | S: Then Venerable Samiddhi rose at the crack of dawn and went to the hot springs to bathe. | T: Then Ven. Samiddhi, as night was ending, got up & went to the Tapodā Hot Springs to bathe his limbs.
sn1.20:1.4 | S: When he had bathed and emerged from the water he stood in one robe drying his limbs. | T: Having bathed his limbs and gotten out of the springs, he stood wearing only his lower robe, letting his limbs dry.
sn1.20:1.5 | S: Then, late at night, a glorious deity, lighting up the entire hot springs, went up to Samiddhi, and, standing in the air, addressed him in verse: | T: Then a certain devatā, in the far extreme of the night, her extreme radiance lighting up the entire Tapodā Hot Springs, went to Ven. Samiddhi. On arrival, while standing in the air, she addressed him with this verse:
sn1.20:2.1 | S: “Mendicant, you seek alms before you eat; | T: “Without having enjoyed (sensual pleasures), you go for alms, monk.
sn1.20:2.2 | S: you don’t seek alms after eating. | T: You don’t go for alms after having enjoyed.
sn1.20:2.3 | S: But you should eat first, then seek alms: | T: Having enjoyed, monk, then go for alms.
sn1.20:2.4 | S: don’t let the time pass you by.” | T: Don’t let time pass you by.”
sn1.20:3.1 | S: “I actually don’t know the time; | T: Ven. Samiddhi: “I don’t know my time.
sn1.20:3.2 | S: it’s hidden and unseen. | T: My time is hidden. It can’t be seen.
sn1.20:3.3 | S: That’s why I seek alms before eating, | T: That’s why, not having enjoyed, I go for alms:
sn1.20:3.4 | S: so that the time may not pass me by!” | T: Don’t let my time pass me by.”
sn1.20:4.1 | S: Then that deity landed on the ground and said to Samiddhi, | T: Then the devatā, coming down to earth, said to Ven. Samiddhi,
sn1.20:4.2 | S: “You’ve gone forth while young, mendicant. With pristine black hair, you’re blessed with youth, in the prime of life, and you’ve never flirted with sensual pleasures. | T: “You have gone forth while young, monk—black-haired, endowed with the blessings of youth in the first stage of life—without having played with sensuality.
sn1.20:4.3 | S: Enjoy human sensual pleasures! | T: Enjoy human sensuality, monk.
sn1.20:4.4 | S: Don’t give up what is apparent in the present to chase after what takes effect over time.” | T: Don’t drop what is visible here & now in pursuit of what’s subject to time.”
sn1.20:5.1 | S: “I’m not, respectable sir. | T: “My friend, I’m not dropping what’s visible here & now in pursuit of what’s subject to time.
sn1.20:5.2 | S: I’m giving up what takes effect over time to chase after what is apparent in the present. | T: I’m dropping what’s subject to time in pursuit of what’s visible here & now.
sn1.20:5.3 | S: For the Buddha has said that sensual pleasures take effect over time, with much suffering and distress, and they’re all the more full of drawbacks. | T: For the Blessed One has said that sensuality is subject to time, of much stress, much despair, & greater drawbacks;
sn1.20:5.4 | S: But this teaching is apparent in the present life, immediately effective, inviting inspection, relevant, so that sensible people can know it for themselves.” | T: whereas this Dhamma is well taught by the Blessed One, visible here & now, timeless, inviting verification, pertinent, to be experienced by the observant for themselves.”
sn1.20:6.1 | S: “But in what way, mendicant, has the Buddha said that sensual pleasures take effect over time, with much suffering and distress, and they’re all the more full of drawbacks? | T: “But, monk, in what way has the Blessed One said that sensuality is subject to time, of much stress, much despair, & greater drawbacks?
sn1.20:6.2 | S: And how is this teaching apparent in the present life, immediately effective, inviting inspection, relevant, so that sensible people can know it for themselves?” | T: And how is this Dhamma visible here & now, timeless, inviting verification, pertinent, to be experienced by the observant for themselves?”
sn1.20:7.1 | S: “I’m junior, respectable sir, recently gone forth, newly come to this teaching and training. | T: “I’m new, my friend, not long gone forth, only recently come to this Dhamma & discipline.
sn1.20:7.2 | S: I’m not able to explain this in detail. | T: I can’t explain it in detail.
sn1.20:7.3 | S: But he, that Blessed One, the perfected one, the fully awakened Buddha, is staying near Rājagaha in the Hot Springs Monastery. | T: But the Blessed One, worthy & rightly self-awakened, is staying here near Rājagaha at Tapodā monastery.
sn1.20:7.4 | S: You should approach him and ask about this matter. | T: Having gone to him, ask him this matter.
sn1.20:7.5 | S: And you should remember it in line with the Buddha’s answer.” | T: As he explains it, that’s how you should remember it.”
sn1.20:8.1 | S: “It’s not easy for us to approach the Buddha, as he is surrounded by other illustrious deities. | T: “Monk, it’s not easy for us to go to the Blessed One, as he is surrounded by other devas of great influence.
sn1.20:8.2 | S: If you go to the Buddha and ask him about this matter, we’ll come along and listen to the teaching.” | T: But if you go to the Blessed One and ask him this matter, I will come along to hear the Dhamma.”
