sn2.23:10.5 | S: ‘I’m a giver, a donor, who praises giving. | T: ‘Here I am, a donor, a lord of giving, one who speaks in praise of giving.
sn2.23:10.6 | S: When they say, “We would give gifts”, what am I to say?’ | T: So when they say, “Let us give gifts,” what am I to say?’
sn2.23:10.7 | S: And so I gave the third gate to my troops. | T: So I gave the third gate to my troops.
sn2.23:10.8 | S: There they gave gifts, while my own giving dwindled. | T: There the troops gave gifts, and my gifts returned to me.
sn2.23:11.1 | S: Then my brahmins and householders approached me and said, | T: “Then brahmans & householders came to me and said,
sn2.23:11.2 | S: ‘Your Majesty gives gifts, the ladies of your harem give gifts, your aristocrat vassals give gifts, your troops give gifts, but we don’t. | T: ‘Your majesty, you give gifts, the harem women give gifts, the noble-warrior subordinates give gifts, the troops give gifts, but we don’t give gifts.
sn2.23:11.3 | S: Your Majesty, please support us to give gifts and make merit.’ | T: It would be good if, depending on you, we gave gifts & did deeds of merit.’
sn2.23:11.4 | S: Then it occurred to me, | T: “Then the thought occurred to me,
sn2.23:11.5 | S: ‘I’m a giver, a donor, who praises giving. | T: ‘Here I am, a donor, a lord of giving, one who speaks in praise of giving.
sn2.23:11.6 | S: When they say, “We would give gifts”, what am I to say?’ | T: So when they say, “Let us give gifts,” what am I to say?’
sn2.23:11.7 | S: And so I gave the fourth gate to my brahmins and householders. | T: So I gave the fourth gate to the brahmans & householders.
sn2.23:11.8 | S: There they gave gifts, while my own giving dwindled. | T: There the brahmans & householders gave gifts, and my gifts returned to me.
sn2.23:12.1 | S: Then my men approached me and said, | T: “Then my men came to me and said,
sn2.23:12.2 | S: ‘Now Your Majesty is not giving gifts anywhere!’ | T: ‘Now your majesty isn’t giving any gifts.’
sn2.23:12.3 | S: When they said this, I said to those men, | T: “When that was said, I said to my men,
sn2.23:12.4 | S: ‘Well then, lads, deposit half the revenue generated in the outer districts in the royal compound. Give the other half as charity to ascetics and brahmins, to paupers, vagrants, supplicants, and beggars in those districts.’ | T: ‘In that case, I say, bring half of the revenue generated in the outlying districts into the city, and give half of it as gifts to contemplatives & brahmans, poor people & travelers, mendicants & beggars.’
sn2.23:12.5 | S: Sir, for a long time I made so much merit and did so many skillful deeds. I never reached any limit so as to say | T: “And so, lord, in the meritorious deeds I did for such a long time, in the skillful deeds I did for such a long time, I didn’t reach a limit of
sn2.23:12.6 | S: ‘there’s this much merit’ or ‘there’s this much result of merit’ or ‘for so long I’ll remain in heaven’. | T: ‘so much merit’ or ‘so much fruit of merit’ or ‘so long I should stay in heaven.’
sn2.23:12.7 | S: It’s incredible, sir, it’s amazing, | T: “How amazing, lord! How astounding!
sn2.23:12.8 | S: how well said this was by the Buddha. | T: How well this was stated by the lord Blessed One!”
sn2.23:13.1 | S: ‘Those who give with faith | T: “‘Those who give it through conviction,
sn2.23:13.2 | S: and a clear and confident heart, | T: with clear, bright awareness:
sn2.23:13.3 | S: partake of food | T: One eats that food,
sn2.23:13.4 | S: in this world and the next. | T: in this world & the next.
sn2.23:14.1 | S: So you should dispel stinginess, | T: So, having subdued miserliness,
sn2.23:14.2 | S: overcoming that stain, and give a gift. | T: one who conquers [that] stain, should give a gift.
sn2.23:14.3 | S: The good deeds of sentient beings | T: Deeds of merit are the support for beings
sn2.23:14.4 | S: support them in the next world.’” | T: when they arise in the other world.’”

## sn22.86
sn22.86:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Vesālī, at the Great Wood, in the hall with the peaked roof. | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Vesāli, in the Great Forest, at the Hall of the Gabled Pavilion.
sn22.86:1.2 | S: Now at that time Venerable Anurādha was staying not far from the Buddha in a wilderness hut. | T: At that time Ven. Anurādha was staying not far from the Blessed One in a wilderness hut.
sn22.86:1.3 | S: Then several wanderers of other religions went up to Venerable Anurādha and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then a large number of wandering sectarians went to Ven. Anurādha and on arrival exchanged courteous greetings with him.
sn22.86:1.4 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, they sat down to one side and said to him: | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, they sat to one side. As they were sitting there, they said to Ven. Anurādha,
sn22.86:1.5 | S: “Reverend Anurādha, when a Realized One is describing a realized one—a supreme person, highest of people, who has reached the highest point—they describe them in these four ways: | T: “Friend Anurādha, the Tathāgata—the supreme man, the superlative man, attainer of the superlative attainment—being described, is described with (one of) these four positions:
sn22.86:1.6 | S: After death, a realized one still exists, or no longer exists, or both still exists and no longer exists, or neither still exists nor no longer exists.” | T: The Tathāgata exists after death, does not exist after death, both does & does not exist after death, neither exists nor does not exist after death.”
sn22.86:2.1 | S: When they said this, Venerable Anurādha said to those wanderers: | T: When this was said, Ven. Anurādha said to the wandering sectarians,
sn22.86:2.2 | S: “Reverends, when a Realized One is describing a realized one—a supreme person, highest of people, who has reached the highest point—they describe them other than these four ways: | T: “Friends, the Tathāgata—the supreme man, the superlative man, attainer of the superlative attainment—being described, is described otherwise than with these four positions:
sn22.86:2.3 | S: After death, a realized one still exists, or no longer exists, or both still exists and no longer exists, or neither still exists nor no longer exists.” | T: The Tathāgata exists after death, does not exist after death, both does & does not exist after death, neither exists nor does not exist after death.”
sn22.86:2.4 | S: When he said this, the wanderers said to him: | T: When this was said, the wandering sectarians said to Ven. Anurādha,
sn22.86:2.5 | S: “This mendicant must be junior, recently gone forth, or else a foolish, incompetent senior mendicant.” | T: “This monk is either a newcomer, not long gone forth, or else an elder who is foolish & inexperienced.”
sn22.86:2.6 | S: Then, after rebuking Venerable Anurādha by calling him “junior” and “foolish”, the wanderers rose from their seats and left. | T: So the wandering sectarians, addressing Ven. Anurādha as they would a newcomer or a fool, got up from their seats and left.
sn22.86:3.1 | S: Soon after they had left, Anurādha thought: | T: Then not long after the wandering sectarians had left, this thought occurred to Ven. Anurādha:
sn22.86:3.2 | S: “If those wanderers were to inquire further, | T: “If I am questioned again by those wandering sectarians,
sn22.86:3.3 | S: how should I answer them so as to repeat what the Buddha has said, and not misrepresent him with an untruth? How should I explain in line with his teaching, so that there would be no legitimate grounds for rebuttal and criticism?” | T: how will I answer in such a way that I will speak in line with what the Blessed One has said, will not misrepresent the Blessed One with what is unfactual, will answer in line with the Dhamma, so that no one whose thinking is in line with the Dhamma will have grounds for criticizing me?”
sn22.86:4.1 | S: Then Venerable Anurādha went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and told him all that had happened. | T: Then Ven. Anurādha went to the Blessed One and on arrival, having bowed down to the Blessed One, sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to the Blessed One:
sn22.86:4.2 | P: “idhāhaṁ, bhante, bhagavato avidūre araññakuṭikāyaṁ viharāmi. | T: “Just now I was staying not far from the Blessed One in a wilderness hut.

