sn22.86:4.3 | P: Atha kho, bhante, sambahulā aññatitthiyā paribbājakā yenāhaṁ tenupasaṅkamiṁsu …pe… maṁ etadavocuṁ: | T: Then a large number of wandering sectarians came and.… said to me,
sn22.86:4.4 | P: ‘yo so, āvuso anurādha, tathāgato uttamapuriso paramapuriso paramapattipatto taṁ tathāgato imesu catūsu ṭhānesu paññāpayamāno paññāpeti— | T: ‘Friend Anurādha, the Tathāgata—the supreme man, the superlative man, attainer of the superlative attainment—being described, is described with (one of) these four positions:
sn22.86:4.5 | P: hoti tathāgato paraṁ maraṇāti vā, na hoti … | T: The Tathāgata exists after death, does not exist after death,
sn22.86:4.6 | P: hoti ca na ca hoti, neva hoti na na hoti tathāgato paraṁ maraṇāti vā’”ti? | T: both does & does not exist after death, neither exists nor does not exist after death.’
sn22.86:5.1 | P: Evaṁ vuttāhaṁ, bhante, te aññatitthiye paribbājake etadavocaṁ: | T: “When this was said, I said to them,
sn22.86:5.2 | P: “yo so, āvuso, tathāgato uttamapuriso paramapuriso paramapattipatto, taṁ tathāgato aññatra imehi catūhi ṭhānehi paññāpayamāno paññāpeti: | T: ‘Friends, the Tathāgata—the supreme man, the superlative man, attainer of the superlative attainment—being described, is described otherwise than with these four positions:
sn22.86:5.3 | P: ‘hoti tathāgato paraṁ maraṇā’ti vā …pe… | T: The Tathāgata exists after death, does not exist after death, both does & does not exist after death,
sn22.86:5.4 | P: ‘neva hoti na na hoti tathāgato paraṁ maraṇā’ti vāti. | T: neither exists nor does not exist after death.’
sn22.86:5.5 | P: Evaṁ vutte, bhante, te aññatitthiyā paribbājakā maṁ etadavocuṁ: | T: “When this was said, the wandering sectarians said to me,
sn22.86:5.6 | P: ‘so cāyaṁ bhikkhu na vo bhavissati acirapabbajito thero vā pana bālo abyatto’ti. | T: ‘This monk is either a newcomer, not long gone forth, or else an elder who is foolish & inexperienced.’
sn22.86:5.7 | P: Atha kho maṁ, bhante, te aññatitthiyā paribbājakā navavādena ca bālavādena ca apasādetvā uṭṭhāyāsanā pakkamiṁsu. | T: So, addressing me as they would a newcomer or a fool, they got up from their seats and left.
sn22.86:6.1 | P: Tassa mayhaṁ, bhante, acirapakkantesu tesu aññatitthiyesu paribbājakesu etadahosi: | T: “Then not long after the wandering sectarians had left, this thought occurred to me:
sn22.86:6.2 | P: ‘sace kho maṁ te aññatitthiyā paribbājakā uttariṁ pañhaṁ puccheyyuṁ. | T: ‘If I am questioned again by those wandering sectarians,
sn22.86:6.3 | P: Kathaṁ byākaramāno nu khvāhaṁ tesaṁ aññatitthiyānaṁ paribbājakānaṁ vuttavādī ceva bhagavato assaṁ, na ca bhagavantaṁ abhūtena abbhācikkheyyaṁ, dhammassa cānudhammaṁ byākareyyaṁ, na ca koci sahadhammiko vādānuvādo gārayhaṁ ṭhānaṁ āgaccheyyā’”ti? | T: how will I answer in such a way that I will speak in line with what the Blessed One has said, will not misrepresent the Blessed One with what is unfactual, will answer in line with the Dhamma, and no one whose thinking is in line with the Dhamma will have grounds for criticizing me?’”
sn22.86:7.1 | S: “What do you think, Anurādha? | T: “What do you think, Anurādha?
sn22.86:7.2 | S: Is form permanent or impermanent?” | T: Is form constant or inconstant?”
sn22.86:7.3 | S: “Impermanent, sir.” | T: “Inconstant, lord.”
sn22.86:7.4 | S: “But if it’s impermanent, is it suffering or happiness?” | T: “And is that which is inconstant easeful or stressful?”
sn22.86:7.5 | S: “Suffering, sir.” | T: “Stressful, lord.”
sn22.86:7.6 | S: “But if it’s impermanent, suffering, and perishable, is it fit to be regarded thus: | T: “And is it proper to regard what is inconstant, stressful, subject to change as:
sn22.86:7.7 | S: ‘This is mine, I am this, this is my self’?” | T: ‘This is mine. This is my self. This is what I am’?”
sn22.86:7.8 | S: “No, sir.” | T: “No, lord.”
sn22.86:7.9 | S: “Is feeling …” … | T: “… Is feeling constant or inconstant?”—“Inconstant, lord.” …
sn22.86:7.10 | S: “… perception …” … | T: “… Is perception constant or inconstant?”—“Inconstant, lord.” …
sn22.86:7.11 | S: “… choices …” … | T: “… Are fabrications constant or inconstant?”—“Inconstant, lord.” …
sn22.86:7.12 | S: “Is consciousness permanent or impermanent?” | T: “Is consciousness constant or inconstant?
sn22.86:7.13 | S: “Impermanent, sir.” … | T: “Inconstant, lord.” “And is that which is inconstant easeful or stressful?” “Stressful, lord.” “And is it proper to regard what is inconstant, stressful, subject to change as: ‘This is mine. This is my self. This is what I am’?” “No, lord.”
sn22.86:7.14 | S: “So you should truly see … | T: “Thus, Anurādha, any form whatsoever that is past, future, or present; internal or external; blatant or subtle; common or sublime; far or near: Every form is to be seen as it has come to be with right discernment as: ‘This is not mine. This is not my self. This is not what I am.’ “Any feeling whatsoever.… “Any perception whatsoever.… “Any fabrications whatsoever.… “Any consciousness whatsoever that is past, future, or present; internal or external; blatant or subtle; common or sublime; far or near: Every consciousness is to be seen as it has come to be with right discernment as: ‘This is not mine. This is not my self. This is not what I am.’
sn22.86:7.15 | S: Seeing this … | T: “Seeing thus, Anurādha, the instructed disciple of the noble ones grows disenchanted with form, disenchanted with feeling, disenchanted with perception, disenchanted with fabrications, disenchanted with consciousness. Disenchanted, he becomes dispassionate. Through dispassion, he is released. With release, there is the knowledge, ‘Released.’
sn22.86:7.16 | S: They understand: ‘… there is nothing further for this place.’ | T: He discerns that ‘Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for this world.’
