## ud4.1
ud4.1:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
ud4.1:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Cālikā, on the Cālikā mountain. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying among the Cālikans, at Cālikā Mountain.
ud4.1:1.3 | S: Now, at that time Venerable Meghiya was the Buddha’s attendant. | T: And on that occasion Ven. Meghiya was his attendant.
ud4.1:1.4 | S: Then Venerable Meghiya went up to the Buddha, bowed, stood to one side, and said to him, | T: Then Ven. Meghiya went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, stood to one side. As he was standing there he said to the Blessed One,
ud4.1:1.5 | S: “Sir, I’d like to enter Jantu village for alms.” | T: “I would like to go into Jantu Village for alms.”
ud4.1:1.6 | S: “Please, Meghiya, go at your convenience.” | T: “Then do, Meghiya, what you think it is now time to do.”
ud4.1:2.1 | S: Then Meghiya robed up in the morning and, taking his bowl and robe, entered Jantu village for alms. | T: Then in the early morning, Ven. Meghiya adjusted his under robe and–carrying his bowl & robes–went into Jantu Village for alms.
ud4.1:2.2 | S: After the meal, on his return from almsround in Jantu village, he went to the shore of Kimikālā River. | T: Having gone for alms in Jantu Village, after the meal, returning from his alms round, he went to the bank of the Kimikālā River.
ud4.1:2.3 | S: As he was going for a walk along the shore of the river he saw a lovely and delightful mango grove. | T: As he was walking up & down along the bank of the river to exercise his legs, he saw a pleasing, charming mango grove.
ud4.1:2.4 | S: Seeing this, it occurred to him, | T: Seeing it, the thought occurred to him:
ud4.1:2.5 | S: “Oh, this mango grove is lovely and delightful! This is good enough for striving for a gentleman wanting to strive. | T: “How pleasing & charming this mango grove! It’s enough for a young man of good family intent on exertion to exert himself [in meditation].
ud4.1:2.6 | S: If the Buddha allows me, I’ll come back to this mango grove to meditate.” | T: If the Blessed One gives me permission, I would like to exert myself [in meditation] in this mango grove.”
ud4.1:3.1 | S: Then Venerable Meghiya went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and told him what had happened, adding, | T: So Ven. Meghiya went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side.
ud4.1:3.2 | P: Ekamantaṁ nisinno kho āyasmā meghiyo bhagavantaṁ etadavoca: | T: As he was sitting there he said to the Blessed One,
ud4.1:4.1 | P: “Idhāhaṁ, bhante, pubbaṇhasamayaṁ nivāsetvā pattacīvaramādāya jantugāmaṁ piṇḍāya pāvisiṁ. | T: “Just now, in the early morning, I adjusted my under robe and–carrying my bowl & robes–went into Jantu Village for alms.
ud4.1:4.2 | P: Jantugāme piṇḍāya caritvā pacchābhattaṁ piṇḍapātapaṭikkanto yena kimikāḷāya nadiyā tīraṁ tenupasaṅkamiṁ. | T: Having gone for alms in Jantu Village, after the meal, returning from my alms round, I went to the bank of the Kimikālā River.
ud4.1:4.3 | P: Addasaṁ kho ahaṁ, bhante, kimikāḷāya nadiyā tīre jaṅghāvihāraṁ anucaṅkamamāno anuvicaramāno ambavanaṁ pāsādikaṁ manuññaṁ ramaṇīyaṁ. | T: As I was walking up & down along the bank of the river to exercise my legs, I saw a pleasing, charming mango grove.
ud4.1:4.4 | P: Disvāna me etadahosi: | T: Seeing it, the thought occurred to me:
ud4.1:4.5 | P: ‘pāsādikaṁ vatidaṁ ambavanaṁ manuññaṁ ramaṇīyaṁ. | T: ‘How pleasing & charming this mango grove!
ud4.1:4.6 | P: Alaṁ vatidaṁ kulaputtassa padhānatthikassa padhānāya. | T: It’s enough for a young man of good family intent on exertion to exert himself [in meditation].
ud4.1:4.7 | P: Sace maṁ bhagavā anujāneyya, āgaccheyyāhaṁ imaṁ ambavanaṁ padhānāyā’ti. | T: If the Blessed One gives me permission, I would like to exert myself [in meditation] in this mango grove.’
ud4.1:4.8 | S: “If the Buddha allows me, I’ll go back to that mango grove to meditate.” | T: If the Blessed One gives me permission, I would like to go to the mango grove to exert myself [in meditation].”
ud4.1:5.1 | S: When he had spoken, the Buddha said to him, | T: When this was said, the Blessed One responded to Ven. Meghiya,
ud4.1:5.2 | S: “We’re alone, Meghiya. Wait until another mendicant comes.” | T: “As long as I am still alone, stay here until another monk comes.”
ud4.1:6.1 | S: For a second time Meghiya said to the Buddha, | T: A second time, Ven. Meghiya said to the Blessed One,
ud4.1:6.2 | S: “Sir, the Buddha has nothing more to do, and nothing that needs improvement. | T: “Lord, the Blessed One has nothing further to do, and nothing further to add to what he has done.
ud4.1:6.3 | S: But I have. | T: I, however, have something further to do, and something further to add to what I have done.
ud4.1:6.4 | S: If you allow me, I’ll go back to that mango grove to meditate.” | T: If the Blessed One gives me permission, I would like to go to the mango grove to exert myself [in meditation].”
ud4.1:6.5 | S: For a second time the Buddha said, | T: A second time, the Blessed One responded to Ven. Meghiya,
ud4.1:6.6 | S: “We’re alone, Meghiya. Wait until another mendicant comes.” | T: “As long as I am still alone, stay here until another monk comes.”
ud4.1:7.1 | S: For a third time Meghiya said to the Buddha, | T: A third time, Ven. Meghiya said to the Blessed One,
ud4.1:7.2 | S: “Sir, the Buddha has nothing more to do, and nothing that needs improvement. | T: “Lord, the Blessed One has nothing further to do, and nothing further to add to what he has done.
ud4.1:7.3 | S: But I have. | T: I, however, have something further to do, and something further to add to what I have done.
ud4.1:7.4 | S: If you allow me, I’ll go back to that mango grove to meditate.” | T: If the Blessed One gives me permission, I would like to go to the mango grove to exert myself [in meditation].”
ud4.1:7.5 | S: “Meghiya, since you speak of striving in meditation, what can I say? | T: “As you are talking about exertion, Meghiya, what can we say?
ud4.1:7.6 | S: Please, Meghiya, go at your convenience.” | T: Do what you think it is now time to do.”
ud4.1:8.1 | S: Then Meghiya got up from his seat, bowed and respectfully circled the Buddha, keeping him on his right. Then he went to that mango grove, and, having plunged deep into it, sat at the root of a certain tree for the day’s meditation. | T: Then Ven. Meghiya, rising from his seat, bowing down to the Blessed One and, circling him to the right, went to the mango grove. On arrival, having gone deep into the grove, he sat down at the root of a certain tree for the day’s abiding.
ud4.1:8.2 | S: But while Meghiya was meditating in that mango grove he was beset mostly by three kinds of bad, unskillful thoughts, namely, | T: Now while Ven. Meghiya was staying in the mango grove, he was for the most part assailed by three kinds of unskillful thoughts:
ud4.1:8.3 | S: sensual, malicious, and cruel thoughts. | T: thoughts of sensuality, thoughts of ill will, and thoughts of doing harm.
ud4.1:9.1 | S: Then he thought, | T: The thought occurred to him:
ud4.1:9.2 | S: “Oh lord, how incredible, how amazing! | T: “How amazing! How astounding!
ud4.1:9.3 | S: I’ve gone forth out of faith from the lay life to homelessness, | T: Even though it was through faith that I went forth from home to the homeless life,
ud4.1:9.4 | S: but I’m still harassed by these three kinds of bad, unskillful thoughts: | T: still I am overpowered by these three kinds of unskillful thoughts:
ud4.1:9.5 | S: sensual, malicious, and cruel thoughts.” | T: thoughts of sensuality, thoughts of ill will, and thoughts of doing harm.”
ud4.1:10.1 | S: Then in the late afternoon, Venerable Meghiya came out of retreat and went to the Buddha. He bowed, sat down to one side, and told the Buddha what had happened. | T: Emerging from his seclusion in the late afternoon, he went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to the Blessed One,
ud4.1:10.2 | P: “idha mayhaṁ, bhante, tasmiṁ ambavane viharantassa yebhuyyena tayo pāpakā akusalā vitakkā samudācaranti, seyyathidaṁ— | T: “Just now, while I was staying in the mango grove, I was for the most part assailed by three kinds of unskillful thoughts:
ud4.1:10.3 | P: kāmavitakko, byāpādavitakko, vihiṁsāvitakko. | T: thoughts of sensuality, thoughts of ill will, and thoughts of doing harm.
ud4.1:10.4 | P: Tassa mayhaṁ, bhante, etadahosi: | T: The thought occurred to me:
ud4.1:10.5 | P: ‘acchariyaṁ vata bho, abbhutaṁ vata bho. | T: ‘How amazing! How astounding!
ud4.1:10.6 | P: Saddhāya ca vatamhā agārasmā anagāriyaṁ pabbajitā. | T: Even though it was through faith that I went forth from home to the homeless life,
ud4.1:10.7 | P: Atha ca panimehi tīhi pāpakehi akusalehi vitakkehi anvāsattā, seyyathidaṁ— | T: still I am overpowered by these three kinds of unskillful thoughts:
ud4.1:10.8 | P: kāmavitakkena, byāpādavitakkena, vihiṁsāvitakkena’”. | T: thoughts of sensuality, thoughts of ill will, and thoughts of doing harm.’”
ud4.1:11.1 | S: “Meghiya, when the heart’s release is not ripe, five things help it ripen. | T: “Meghiya, in one whose awareness-release is still immature, five qualities bring it to maturity.
ud4.1:11.2 | S: What five? | T: Which five?
ud4.1:12.1 | S: Firstly, a mendicant has good friends, companions, and associates. | T: “There is the case where a monk has admirable people as friends, companions, & colleagues.
ud4.1:12.2 | S: This is the first thing … | T: In one whose awareness-release is still immature, this is the first quality that brings it to maturity.
ud4.1:13.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant is ethical, restrained in the monastic code, conducting themselves well and resorting for alms in suitable places. Seeing danger in the slightest fault, they keep the rules they’ve undertaken. | T: “Furthermore, the monk is virtuous. He dwells restrained in accordance with the Pāṭimokkha, consummate in his behavior & range of activity. He trains himself, having undertaken the training rules, seeing danger in the slightest faults.
ud4.1:13.2 | S: This is the second thing … | T: In one whose awareness-release is still immature, this is the second quality that brings it to maturity.
ud4.1:14.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant gets to take part in talk about self-effacement that helps open the heart and leads solely to disillusionment, dispassion, cessation, peace, insight, awakening, and extinguishment when they want, without trouble or difficulty. That is, talk about fewness of wishes, contentment, seclusion, aloofness, arousing energy, ethics, immersion, wisdom, freedom, and the knowledge and vision of freedom. | T: “Furthermore, he gets to hear at will, easily and without difficulty, talk that is truly sobering and conducive to the opening of awareness, i.e., talk on modesty, contentment, seclusion, non-entanglement, arousing persistence, virtue, concentration, discernment, release, and the knowledge & vision of release.
ud4.1:14.2 | S: This is the third thing … | T: In one whose awareness-release is still immature, this is the third quality that brings it to maturity.
ud4.1:15.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant lives with energy roused up for giving up unskillful qualities and embracing skillful qualities. They are strong, staunchly vigorous, not slacking off when it comes to developing skillful qualities. | T: “Furthermore, he keeps his persistence aroused for abandoning unskillful [mental] qualities and for taking on skillful qualities. He is steadfast, solid in his effort, not shirking his duties with regard to skillful qualities.
ud4.1:15.2 | S: This is the fourth thing … | T: In one whose awareness-release is still immature, this is the fourth quality that brings it to maturity.
ud4.1:16.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant is wise. They have the wisdom of arising and passing away which is noble, penetrative, and leads to the complete ending of suffering. | T: “Furthermore, he is discerning, endowed with the discernment related to arising & passing away–noble, penetrating, leading to the right ending of stress.
ud4.1:16.2 | S: This is the fifth thing that, when the heart’s release is not ripe, helps it ripen. | T: In one whose awareness-release is still immature, this is the fifth quality that brings it to maturity.
ud4.1:16.3 | S: These are the five things that, when the heart’s release is not ripe, help it ripen. | T: “Meghiya, in one whose awareness-release is still immature, these are the five qualities that bring it to maturity.
ud4.1:17.1 | S: A mendicant with good friends, companions, and associates can expect to be ethical … | T: “Meghiya, when a monk has admirable people as friends, companions, & colleagues, it is to be expected that he will be virtuous, will dwell restrained in accordance with the Pāṭimokkha, consummate in his behavior & range of activity, and will train himself, having undertaken the training rules, seeing danger in the slightest faults.
ud4.1:18.1 | S: A mendicant with good friends, companions, and associates can expect to take part in talk about self-effacement that helps open the heart … | T: “When a monk has admirable people as friends & colleagues, it is to be expected that he will get to hear at will, easily and without difficulty, talk that is truly sobering and conducive to the opening of awareness, i.e., talk on modesty, contentment, seclusion, non-entanglement, arousing persistence, virtue, concentration, discernment, release, and the knowledge & vision of release.
ud4.1:19.1 | S: A mendicant with good friends, companions, and associates can expect to be energetic … | T: “When a monk has admirable people as friends, companions, & colleagues, it is to be expected that he will keep his persistence aroused for abandoning unskillful qualities and for taking on skillful qualities–steadfast, solid in his effort, not shirking his duties with regard to skillful qualities.
ud4.1:20.1 | S: A mendicant with good friends, companions, and associates can expect to be wise … | T: “When a monk has admirable people as friends, companions, & colleagues, it is to be expected that he will be discerning, endowed with the discernment relating to arising & passing away–noble, penetrating, leading to the right ending of stress.
ud4.1:21.1 | S: But then, a mendicant grounded on these five things should develop four further things. | T: “And furthermore, when the monk is established in these five qualities, there are four additional qualities he should develop:
ud4.1:21.2 | S: They should develop the perception of ugliness to give up greed, love to give up hate, mindfulness of breathing to cut off thinking, and perception of impermanence to uproot the conceit ‘I am’. | T: He should develop [contemplation of] the unattractive so as to abandon passion. He should develop good will so as to abandon ill will. He should develop mindfulness of in-&-out breathing so as to cut off thinking. He should develop the perception of inconstancy so as to uproot the conceit, ‘I am.’
ud4.1:21.3 | S: When you perceive impermanence, the perception of not-self becomes stabilized. | T: For a monk perceiving inconstancy, the perception of not-self is made steady.
ud4.1:21.4 | S: Perceiving not-self, you uproot the conceit ‘I am’ and attain extinguishment in this very life.” | T: One perceiving not-self attains the uprooting of the conceit, ‘I am’–unbinding right in the here-&-now.”
ud4.1:22.1 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: Then, on realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion exclaimed:
ud4.1:23.1 | S: “Lesser thoughts and subtle thoughts | T: Little thoughts, subtle thoughts,
ud4.1:23.2 | S: arise, springing up in the mind. | T: when followed, stir up the heart.
ud4.1:23.3 | S: Not understanding these thoughts, | T: Not comprehending the thoughts of the heart,
ud4.1:23.4 | S: one with mind astray runs from one thing to the next. | T: one runs here & there, the mind out of control.
ud4.1:24.1 | S: Having understood these thoughts | T: But comprehending the thoughts of the heart,
ud4.1:24.2 | S: that arise, springing up in the mind, | T: (none)
ud4.1:24.3 | S: an awakened one—keen, restrained, and mindful— | T: one who is ardent, mindful, restrains them. When, followed, they stir up the heart, one awakened
ud4.1:24.4 | S: has given them up without remainder.” | T: lets them go without trace.
ud4.1:24.5 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)


